A||||7||phiero||How to run battery cables?||||1||
Z||000000||phiero||12-15-2001||12:11 PM||||I am moving my battery up front, Is there any specific place I should run the cables?<BR>What is the safest? What is the easiest? I have about 15 feet of flexible conduit, should I run the cable in that for added protection?<P>-phiero from philadelphia ||||reg||1
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-15-2001||01:25 PM||||I remember someone said before they ran theirs inside a garden hose (cheap plastic or rubber to protect it)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jefrysuko||12-15-2001||03:38 PM||||The conduit should work well.  Most people run them along side the fuel tank.  I have even heard of one who dropped the tank to put them above so that he wouldn't have to protect them.  I will probably do one on each side of the tank inside a garden hose or plastic conduit when I get around to it myself.<P>Have fun!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-15-2001||04:27 PM||||Anything that protects them is a plus. If it's something flexible, make sure it can't sag where things could hit it or worse, hook onto it.<P>I'd use something ridgid myself... that way they can't sag between anchor points. sagging cables would be bad.... mmK... Plastic conduit is made in gray. that wouldn't stand out like PVC white pipe and is tougher. That heavy black plastic tubing, commonly used for sprinkler work, is fairly stiff and abrasion resistant.<P>The last thing you want is for something to be able to rub them thru.... If they short to the tank itself they could easily burn a hole right thru. You're dealing with something that can generate 200-400 amps across a short...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||12-15-2001||09:20 PM||||*USE ONLY HARD GREY UNDERGROUND ELECTRICAL CONDIUT!!**<P>YEIKES!  Do NOT use garden hose! ****  ****  **** !<P>You're putting a 1,000A arc welder half an inch away from a thinwall steel gasolene tank.....<P>The condiut works great, is nearly industructable, and will out-last the car.  Don't use the flex stuff, it's better than hose, but not by a lot.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||fix||12-15-2001||10:04 PM||||I used to work for an electrical company.   I figure UA or EF 3/4" flexible conduit would work pretty well and is rather durable too.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||12-15-2001||10:14 PM||||I used grey schedule 80 PVC electrical conduit clamped to the the coolant tube on the passenger side.  I put a spacer between the PVC and the tube so that they are not in direct contact.  Where the tube has several bends beginning at the rear of the front wheelwell, I used flexible conduit and spaced it off the the tubing as well.  Work out nice, you can't see it, and the cable is well protected.<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||12-16-2001||11:47 AM||||I ran mine through the center console but I think I'm going to change to a setup where they run through metal conduit strapped to the coolant pipes underneath the car. <P>There's no moving parts that touch the cable<BR>where it is now, but someone on the forum pointed out that it is right above the gas tank, and I've been thinking that it might be a smart idea to get it farther away from the tank.||||reg||1
